% ============================================================================
% prefix-meaning — common English prefixes and what they actually mean
% (adj-facts-stdlib, LANGUAGE).
% ============================================================================
% A prefix attaches to the front of a base word and changes its meaning —
% "un-" in "unimportant" doesn't name a thing, it negates the word it
% attaches to. Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? prefix_meaning(un_, $M)
%       % negation_or_absence
%
% A native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5): each `row` lowers to a ground
% relation `prefix_meaning(prefix, meaning)` carrying the source citation,
% so the lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query — the engine
% returns the meaning WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero model calls,
% and abstains on any prefix not one of these six source-cited examples.
% The query also runs BACKWARD — bind a meaning and recall which prefix
% carries it:
%
%     ? prefix_meaning($P, doing_again)
%       % re_
%
% DESIGN NOTE — why `prefix` is a TRAILING-UNDERSCORE atom label rather
% than the punctuated form: ADJ atom labels cannot contain hyphens, so
% this table introduces a NEW atom-label convention for this stdlib — a
% trailing underscore marks that the atom is a prefix, attaching to the
% FRONT of a word (`un_` stands for "un-"). This mirrors how
% `idiom-meaning.adj`/`simile-meaning.adj` established underscore-joined
% atom labels for multi-word phrases that can't otherwise be represented;
% here the underscore instead marks word-attachment position. The header
% prose below quotes the ORIGINAL punctuated form, so the mapping remains
% independently checkable against the cited page.
%
% Provenance: quoted verbatim from Grammarly's "Prefixes: Definition and
% Examples" article, which pairs each prefix with its meaning and an
% example word directly:
%
%     prefix (this table's atom label)   meaning                  example word   meaning quote (verbatim)
%     -----------------------------------  -----------------------  -------------  -------------------------------
%     un_                                   negation_or_absence      unimportant    "negation or the absence of"
%     re_                                   doing_again              repeat         "doing something again"
%     dis_                                  negation_or_reversal     disapprove     "negation or reversal"
%     non_                                  not_or_negation          nonstop        "not or negation"
%     pre_                                  happening_before         predict        "happening before"
%     inter_                                among_between            interact       "among, between"
%
% WebFetch-verified before writing (twice — the second pass re-fetched the
% same page's full prefix table to pull `non_`/`pre_`/`inter_` alongside
% the three rows already shipped, confirming each is its own clean,
% single-fact short phrase in the source's own table, not a bundled
% multi-sense entry). `trust consensus` — Grammarly is a reputable,
% widely-cited grammar-content company, the same tier this stdlib already
% reserves for other non-.gov language sources and the same source family
% already cited by `sentence-type.adj`/`part-of-speech.adj`/
% `contraction.adj`/`possessive-noun.adj`/`simile-meaning.adj`. Extends
% the already-shipped three-row table (un_/re_/dis_) with three more
% prefixes from the SAME already-cited source page, picked for genuinely
% distinct semantic categories rather than near-synonyms of the negation
% family already covered: `non_` (negation, reinforcing but distinct from
% un_/dis_), `pre_` (temporal — before), `inter_` (relational — among/
% between). Picked using the mandatory full-tree-grep-before-scoping
% discipline before this cycle's research pivoted here (an unrelated
% `homograph` candidate was researched first and abandoned — every source
% tried bundled both senses of a homograph word into one sentence rather
% than stating one clean meaning per sentence). A prefix not one of these
% six — e.g. `over_`, a real, well-known prefix the SAME source page also
% covers, but deliberately still not a row here — is NOT a row, so a
% recall ABSTAINS on it rather than inventing a meaning. (See
% ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table prefix_meaning {
    columns prefix, meaning

    row (un_, negation_or_absence)
    row (re_, doing_again)
    row (dis_, negation_or_reversal)
    row (non_, not_or_negation)
    row (pre_, happening_before)
    row (inter_, among_between)

    source "negation or the absence of"
    locator "https://www.grammarly.com/blog/parts-of-speech/prefixes/"
    trust consensus
}
